If Equals says two values are equal, GetHashCode must return the same code for them in that execution. Different values may still share a hash; a hash collision is not proof of equality.

Equals(other) tests logical equality and returns a Boolean. GetHashCode() takes no arguments and returns a hash code; compare equal values’ hashes rather than expecting a fixed number:
```csharp
using System;
var a = new SampleKey("Read");
var b = new SampleKey("Read");
Console.WriteLine(a.Equals(b));
Console.WriteLine(a.Equals(new SampleKey("Write")));
Console.WriteLine(a.GetHashCode() == b.GetHashCode());
public record SampleKey(string Name);
```
Output: True, then False, then True.

Complete local C# example; the app does not execute it:
```csharp
using System;
using System.Collections.Generic;
var first = new TopicKey("C#");
var second = new TopicKey("C#");
var topics = new HashSet<TopicKey> { first, second };
Console.WriteLine(topics.Count);
public record TopicKey(string Name);
```

